    "video": null,
    "html": "<p>If you are building a remote MCP server with OAuth, the first thing that\nfails is usually the redirect URI. Claude, ChatGPT and Claude Code each send\nusers back to a different place, and your authorization server has to decide\nwhich of those it trusts. Here is what each one sends, why the decision\nmatters, and what we did on two servers we run.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-each-client-sends\">What each client sends</h2>\n<p><strong>Hosted Claude apps</strong> (claude.ai on the web, Desktop, mobile, Cowork) use\nexactly one callback:</p>\n<pre><code>https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Claude Code</strong> is a CLI, so it uses an RFC 8252 loopback redirect on an\nephemeral port. Its Client ID Metadata Document declares\n<code>http://localhost/callback</code> and <code>http://127.0.0.1/callback</code>, and the port\nchanges every login. Match loopback with the port ignored, for both\nhostnames.</p>\n<p><strong>ChatGPT</strong> gives each app its own callback, shown on the app management\npage:</p>\n<pre><code>https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/{callback_id}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The older <code>https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect</code> is still\nvalid for published apps.</p>\n<p>A few more things from Claude&#39;s connector docs that bite people. Claude\nsupports both Dynamic Client Registration (DCR) and Client ID Metadata\nDocuments (CIMD) by default, and picks CIMD only when your authorization\nserver metadata advertises <code>client_id_metadata_document_supported: true</code> and\n<code>&quot;none&quot;</code> in <code>token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported</code>. It starts sign-in only if\nyour MCP endpoint answers 401 with a <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> header carrying\n<code>resource_metadata</code>, and ignores that header on a 200. It uses only the first\nentry in <code>authorization_servers</code>, requires S256 PKCE, and gives discovery,\nregistration and token calls 10 seconds (refresh gets 30).</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-have-an-allowlist-at-all\">Why have an allowlist at all</h2>\n<p>With open Dynamic Client Registration, anyone can register a client with any\nredirect URI. PKCE does not fix that. PKCE stops someone redeeming a code they\ndid not start, but an attacker who registers their own server as the redirect\nis the party that started the flow. They send a victim an authorize link, the\nvictim approves, and the code lands on the attacker&#39;s server along with the\nverifier they chose.</p>\n<p>So something has to decide which redirect URIs are acceptable. Every hosted\nentry you allow is a party you trust with your users&#39; authorization codes, so\nkeep the list short and exact.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-our-search-mcp-server-allows\">What our search MCP server allows</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://aeo.advancelabs.dev/mcp\">Advance Labs AEO Toolkit</a> search MCP server (changed 2026-10-31) accepts\nloopback on any port, plus exactly three hosted entries. A sketch of the\nlogic:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-ts\">const LOOPBACK = new Set([&quot;localhost&quot;, &quot;127.0.0.1&quot;, &quot;[::1]&quot;]);\n\nfunction allowed(raw: string): boolean {\n  let u: URL;\n  try { u = new URL(raw); } catch { return false; }\n  if (u.hash || u.username || u.password) return false;\n\n  // Claude Code: http loopback, any port, nothing else\n  if (u.protocol === &quot;http:&quot;) return LOOPBACK.has(u.hostname);\n  if (u.protocol !== &quot;https:&quot; || u.port) return false;\n  if (u.search) return false;\n\n  // Hosted entries: exact host, exact path\n  if (u.hostname === &quot;claude.ai&quot;) {\n    return u.pathname === &quot;/api/mcp/auth_callback&quot;;\n  }\n  if (u.hostname === &quot;chatgpt.com&quot;) {\n    return (\n      u.pathname === &quot;/connector_platform_oauth_redirect&quot; ||\n      /^\\/connector\\/oauth\\/[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$/.test(u.pathname)\n    );\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The interesting part is the refusals. We have tests for each of these near\nmisses, and all of them are rejected:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>http</code> instead of <code>https</code> on a hosted entry</li>\n<li>an extra path segment after the callback</li>\n<li>any query string</li>\n<li>any non-default port</li>\n<li>subdomains, such as <code>evil.claude.ai</code></li>\n<li>lookalike hosts, such as <code>claude.ai.evil.example</code></li>\n<li>two path segments, or an empty one, after <code>/connector/oauth/</code></li>\n<li>encoded traversal like <code>..%2F</code></li>\n<li>fragments</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ChatGPT per-app pattern is the one to be careful with. It has to accept\nwhatever <code>callback_id</code> OpenAI generates, so it allows one segment of\n<code>[A-Za-z0-9_-]+</code> and nothing else. That is still a wildcard, and it is a\nwildcard on a host you decided to trust.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-runs-ondev-had-and-what-we-changed\">What runs-on.dev had, and what we changed</h2>\n<p>runs-on.dev has its own MCP server (<code>claude mcp add --transport http runs-on https://runs-on.dev/.well-known/mcp</code>, then sign in with GitHub). Writing this\npost is how we noticed it had the open variant: registration accepted any\n<code>https</code> redirect URI, plus <code>http</code> on loopback. The claude.ai and chatgpt.com\ncallbacks worked because Claude and ChatGPT register them, but so did any other\n<code>https</code> address, on a server whose codes can change a user&#39;s DNS records.</p>\n<p>It had mitigations. Protocol errors only redirected back to loopback, so the\nauthorize endpoint was never an open redirect. The consent page showed the\nsigned-in GitHub login and the host you were being sent to. PKCE with S256 was\nrequired. None of that stops a user who clicks Approve on a page naming a host\nthey do not recognise.</p>\n<p>So the same day, runs-on.dev got the same allowlist as above: loopback, the\nClaude callback, and the two ChatGPT ones. Registering any other redirect now\nreturns 400. The code is in <code>lib/oauth.js</code>.</p>\n<p>One detail is worth copying if your client ids are stateless. runs-on.dev\nissues a client id that is the signed registration itself, with no database.\nThat means a client registered before the change still carries its old\nredirect, and its signature still verifies. Checking the allowlist only at\n<code>/register</code> would have left every earlier registration working forever. The\ncheck now runs again at <code>/authorize</code>, and there is a test that builds a\npre-change client id with an arbitrary <code>https</code> redirect and confirms it is\nrefused.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Claude web, Desktop, mobile: <code>https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback</code>.</li>\n<li>Claude Code: loopback on <code>localhost</code> and <code>127.0.0.1</code>, port ignored.</li>\n<li>ChatGPT: <code>https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/{callback_id}</code>, plus the legacy\n<code>connector_platform_oauth_redirect</code>.</li>\n<li>Compare scheme, host, port, path and query exactly. Do not use prefix or\nsuffix matching on hostnames.</li>\n<li>Return a 401 with <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> and <code>resource_metadata</code>, advertise S256,\nand keep your discovery and token endpoints fast.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On 2026-10-31 we published the AEO Toolkit servers to the official MCP Registry\n(<a href=\"https://docs.advancelabs.dev/aeo-toolkit/reference/mcp-registry-install/\">install guide</a>, and\n<a href=\"https://advancelabs.dev/guides/where-to-list-your-mcp-server\">how we listed them</a>)\nas <code>dev.advancelabs/aeo-search</code>, <code>aeo-visibility</code> and <code>aeo-backlink</code>. Directory\nreviewers also want every tool to carry a title and\n<code>readOnlyHint</code>/<code>destructiveHint</code> annotations, so we made that field required\nin our shared tool type. The sources for the client behavior above are\n<a href=\"https://claude.com/docs/connectors/building/authentication\">Claude&#39;s connector authentication docs</a>\nand <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/apps-sdk/build/auth\">OpenAI&#39;s Apps SDK auth guide</a>.</p>\n",
