    "status": "published",
    "image": null,
    "video": null,
    "html": "<p>Until now the <a href=\"/.well-known/mcp\">MCP server</a> could only look things up: is a\nname free, and what does a claimed name point at. Today it can change things\ntoo. Connect your coding agent and it can repoint your name, add verification\nrecords, publish a site, and roll it back, without you opening /manage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"set-it-up-one-command-one-browser-click\">Set it up: one command, one browser click</h2>\n<p>Add the server to your agent.</p>\n<p>Claude Code:</p>\n<pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http runs-on https://runs-on.dev/.well-known/mcp\n</code></pre>\n<p>Codex:</p>\n<pre><code>codex mcp add runs-on --url https://runs-on.dev/.well-known/mcp\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then sign in: <code>/mcp</code> → <strong>Authenticate</strong> in Claude Code, or\n<code>codex mcp login runs-on</code> in Codex. A browser opens on runs-on.dev, you sign\nin with GitHub as usual, and you pick what the agent gets: deploys only, or\ndeploys plus your DNS. That&#39;s it. No token to copy anywhere.</p>\n<p>Then ask for what you want: &quot;point my name at my Vercel project&quot;, &quot;add the\n<code>_discord</code> TXT record Discord gave me&quot;, &quot;deploy <code>dist/</code> and give me the URL&quot;,\n&quot;the last deploy broke the homepage, roll it back&quot;. The full walkthrough is in\nthe <a href=\"/docs/mcp\">MCP guide</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-agent-gets\">What the agent gets</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>tool</th>\n<th>token</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>whoami</code></td>\n<td>any</td>\n<td>your login, the token scope, the names you own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>update_records</code></td>\n<td>manage</td>\n<td>apex records, subdomains, profile card</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>deploy_site</code></td>\n<td>any</td>\n<td>publish static files to your name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>list_deployments</code></td>\n<td>any</td>\n<td>history, and which deployment is live</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rollback_site</code></td>\n<td>any</td>\n<td>instantly re-point at an earlier deployment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>check_name</code> and <code>get_record</code> are still there and still need no token.</p>\n<h2 id=\"same-rules-one-more-front-door\">Same rules, one more front door</h2>\n<p>The agent does not get a separate code path. A record edit over MCP runs the\nsame validation as the /manage editor and as CI on a pull request, lands as\nthe same public commit in the registry, and syncs to DNS the same way. Deploys\nand rollbacks share code with the HTTP deploy API, and each tool spends the\nsame per-account rate limit the website does. If an agent sends a CNAME\nalongside an A record, it gets the same error you would.</p>\n<h2 id=\"signing-in-without-a-new-account-system\">Signing in, without a new account system</h2>\n<p>The login is standard MCP OAuth: your agent discovers runs-on.dev&#39;s\nauthorization server, registers itself, and sends you to an approval page\nwith PKCE guarding the handoff. Behind it is the GitHub sign-in the site\nalready uses, and what comes out is the same kind of token /manage hands\nout. So every agent login shows up in your key list, expires after 30 days,\nand stops working the moment you delete it.</p>\n<p>For CI or anywhere without a browser, you can still generate a token on\n/manage and pass it as an <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header. The\n<a href=\"/docs/mcp\">MCP guide</a> has both.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-dns-is-a-separate-permission\">Why DNS is a separate permission</h2>\n<p>Deploy tokens already live in people&#39;s CI. Quietly letting every existing token\nedit DNS would have handed new power to keys minted for something narrower. So\nediting DNS is a separate choice, made on the approval page or with the\n<strong>also edit DNS</strong> box at mint time, and the key list on /manage shows which\nkind each key is.</p>\n<p>Three things stay out of reach on purpose: claiming, swapping, and releasing a\nname. Those are hard to undo, so they stay behind your GitHub sign-in in the\nbrowser.</p>\n",
